.Dd November 8, 2026
.Dt NIGHTSAVER 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nightsaver
.Nd OLED-friendly Xorg screensaver and PAM screen locker
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl display Ar host:display.screen
.Op Fl verbose
.Op Fl quiet
.Op Fl no-splash
.Op Fl log Ar file
.Op Fl debug
.Op Fl sync
.Op Fl Fl lock | Fl Fl no-lock
.Op Fl Fl config Ar file
.Op Fl Fl pattern Ar name
.Op Fl Fl backend Cm auto | x11 | wayland
.Nm
.Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a screensaver daemon for the X Window System that is designed as a drop-in
replacement for the
.Xr xscreensaver 1
daemon.
It accepts the same daemon flags, answers the same control protocol (so the
real
.Xr xscreensaver-command 1
works against it), and reads the same
.Pa ~/.xscreensaver
resource file for the settings it understands.
It does not run external xscreensaver hack programs; it draws a small set of
built-in patterns into a software framebuffer instead.
Every frame passes through a brightness cap and a burn-in protection stage
intended for OLED panels.
.Pp
After the idle timeout the screen is blanked.
If locking is enabled, the screen is locked after the lock timeout and a PAM
password is required to unlock it.
Only the Xorg server is supported.
The required server extensions are MIT-SCREEN-SAVER, XInputExtension 2.x,
XKEYBOARD and RANDR.
DPMS, MIT-SHM, XFIXES and XINERAMA are optional.
.Pp
Only one screensaver may manage a display at a time.
.Nm
refuses to start if another daemon (including
.Xr xscreensaver 1 )
already owns the display.
.Sh OPTIONS
Options may be given with one or two leading dashes.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl display Ar dpy
Also
.Fl dpy
and
.Fl disp .
The X display to manage.
The default is
.Ev DISPLAY .
.It Fl Fl backend Cm auto | x11 | wayland
The display system to use.
.Cm auto ,
the default, selects Wayland when
.Ev WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set and X11 otherwise.
There is no Wayland backend yet, so selecting it makes
.Nm
exit with an error.
.Cm x11
in a Wayland session runs on XWayland with locking disabled: the screen is
blanked, but
.Nm
never locks and never reports a lock, because X grabs do not reach native
Wayland clients.
.It Fl verbose
Also
.Fl v .
Increase log verbosity.
May be repeated, or written
.Fl vv ,
.Fl vvv ,
.Fl vvvv .
.It Fl quiet
Suppress informational output (verbosity 0).
.It Fl no-splash
Also
.Fl nosplash .
Do not show a splash screen.
This is the default, as
.Nm
has no splash screen.
.It Fl splash
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.It Fl log Ar file
Append log messages to
.Ar file .
.It Fl debug
Enable debugging output (raises verbosity to at least 2).
.It Fl sync
Also
.Fl synch ,
.Fl synchronize ,
.Fl synchronise .
Run X synchronously.
.It Fl Fl lock
Lock the screen when blanking, overriding the configuration files.
.It Fl Fl no-lock
Do not lock the screen when blanking, overriding the configuration files.
.It Fl Fl config Ar file
Read this TOML file instead of
.Pa ~/.config/nightsaver/config.toml .
The file must exist.
.It Fl Fl pattern Ar name
Show only the named pattern (mode
.Dq one ) .
See
.Xr nightsaver.toml 5
for the pattern names.
.It Fl Fl demo-blank
Debugging aid: map black windows on every monitor for three seconds and exit.
.It Fl version
Also
.Fl ver ,
.Fl vers ,
.Fl Fl version .
Print the version, and the xscreensaver version it is compatible with, and exit.
.It Fl help
Also
.Fl Fl help ,
.Fl h .
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Pp
Invalid options print a message to standard error and exit with status 1.
.Sh BEHAVIOUR
.Ss Idle, blank, lock
.Nm
polls the X server's idle time once per second.
When the user has been idle for the configured
.Va timeout
the screen is blanked: a black override-redirect window is mapped over every
monitor, the cursor is hidden and the keyboard and pointer are grabbed.
The grab is retried for several seconds if another client holds one.
The screen is never locked, and the unlock dialog never shown, without a
successful keyboard and pointer grab.
.Pp
While the screen is blanked but not locked, any key press, button press, or
cumulative pointer movement beyond
.Va motion_threshold_px
unblanks it.
.Pp
If locking is enabled, the screen becomes locked
.Va lock_timeout
after blanking (immediately if that is zero).
It also locks immediately on
.Li nightsaver-command -lock ,
which works even when locking is disabled in the configuration.
While locked, the first key press or pointer movement wakes the display and
opens an unlock dialog (that first key press is not used as password input).
The user types the password and presses Return.
Escape, or
.Va passwd_timeout
seconds without input, hides the dialog again.
A failed attempt shows an error for two seconds and imposes a delay before the
next attempt: 1, 2, 4, 8 seconds, then 10 seconds for each further failure.
A successful PAM result unblanks the screen.
.Ss Patterns
.Nm
displays one of the built-in patterns, chosen according to
.Va mode ,
and changes pattern every
.Va cycle
seconds.
.Fl select Ar N
of
.Xr nightsaver-command 1
addresses the configured pattern list, 1-based.
.Ss DPMS
If DPMS management is enabled
.Pq Va dpms.enabled ,
then on blanking
.Nm
saves the server's DPMS timeouts, installs the configured standby, suspend and
off timeouts and enables DPMS.
With
.Va dpms.quick_off
the monitor is powered off immediately.
On unblanking the monitor is forced on and the saved settings are restored.
.Li nightsaver-command -suspend
powers the monitor off immediately.
.Pp
On an X server without the DPMS extension (framebuffer drivers such as scfb,
and Xvfb) the monitor cannot be powered off.
.Nm
then shows a pure black screen instead: after the shortest non-zero configured
timeout, immediately with
.Va dpms.quick_off ,
and on
.Li nightsaver-command -suspend .
Activity unblanks as usual; while locked, a key press or pointer movement
brings the pattern and the unlock dialog back and restarts the timeout.
The
.Dq NOT LOCKED
notice of a pending lock is always shown.
.Pp
DPMS has not yet been tested on real hardware.
.Ss Configuration reload
The configuration files are checked every five seconds, and reloaded when
their modification time changes or when
.Nm
receives
.Dv SIGHUP .
A file that fails to parse is reported in the log and the previous settings
are kept.
A reload never changes the lock state of a screen that is already locked.
.Ss Signals
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv SIGTERM , SIGINT"
.It Dv SIGHUP
Reload the configuration.
.It Dv SIGTERM , SIGINT
Shut down cleanly.
Both are ignored, with a message in the log, while the screen is locked.
.It Dv SIGPIPE
Ignored.
.El
.Pp
Do not use
.Dv SIGKILL
on a running locker.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa ~/.config/nightsaver/config.toml
Per-user configuration;
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is honoured.
See
.Xr nightsaver.toml 5 .
.It Pa ~/.xscreensaver
xscreensaver resource file; a subset of keys is read.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/nightsaver.toml.sample
Annotated sample configuration.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/pam.d/xscreensaver
PAM service used by default.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/pam.d/nightsaver
Optional PAM service, used only when
.Va pam_service
is set to
.Dq nightsaver .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Usage errors, a failure to open the display and the presence of another
screensaver all produce status 1.
The daemon runs until it receives the
.Cm exit
command or
.Dv SIGTERM
(0 status), or a fatal X error occurs.
.Sh SECURITY
Authentication uses OpenPAM, in a separate helper process
.Pa libexec/nightsaver-auth
that
.Nm
starts for each unlock attempt and feeds the password over a pipe.
Only a clean exit status 0 of the helper unlocks; a crash, a signal or a helper that
does not finish within 25 seconds (it is then killed) counts as a denial.
.Nm
and the helper run as the invoking user and are
.Em not
setuid.
Configuration files must be regular files owned by the user or root and not writable by
group or others; otherwise they are ignored with an error, because they select the PAM
service.
While a lock is pending (another client holds an input grab), the screen stays blanked
with a visible
.Dq NOT LOCKED
notice and
.Fl deactivate ,
.Fl exit ,
.Fl restart
and
.Dv SIGTERM
are refused, exactly as when locked.
The default PAM service is
.Dq xscreensaver ;
its policy uses
.Xr pam_exec 8
to run the setuid helper
.Pa unix-selfauth-helper ,
which verifies the calling user's own password, so no privilege is needed in
.Nm
itself.
Any PAM error is treated as a failed unlock, never as success.
Password buffers are zeroed after use and neither passwords nor key input are
logged.
.Pp
While the screen is locked:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
The windows are never unmapped, and the daemon never exits, except after a
successful PAM result.
.It
The
.Cm exit
and
.Cm restart
commands are refused, and
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGINT
are ignored.
.It
The windows are raised again if they are covered, and the grabs are
re-established if lost.
.El
.Pp
As with
.Xr xscreensaver 1 ,
a lock screen on X11 is only as strong as the X server.
It cannot defend against:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the X server being killed, for example with Ctrl-Alt-Backspace if the keymap
enables it (this returns to the login manager, or to a console);
.It
switching to a virtual terminal where a login session is open (Ctrl-Alt-Fn);
.It
other X clients that were able to grab the input devices before
.Nm ,
or X server debugging or "kill" key combinations;
.It
anyone with root access, or access to the user's X authority.
.El
.Pp
If virtual terminals are a concern, make sure no unlocked console session is
left logged in.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nightsaver-command 1 ,
.Xr xscreensaver 1 ,
.Xr xscreensaver-command 1 ,
.Xr pam 3 ,
.Xr nightsaver.toml 5
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Lucas Holt
.Sh BUGS
Only Xorg is supported; there is no Wayland backend yet.
In a Wayland session
.Nm
refuses to start unless
.Fl Fl backend Cm x11
is given, and then it cannot lock.
Running xscreensaver hack programs, the xscreensaver settings dialog, fading
(the
.Va fade
and
.Va unfade
resources are parsed but ignored) and D-Bus or systemd inhibition are not
implemented.
Multi-monitor hotplug and DPMS on real hardware have not yet been tested.
